WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:08.100
Hilary Swank won fame and acclaim for her
Oscar-winning performance in 1999's Boys Don't

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:12.380
Cry, and did it all over again just five years
later in Million Dollar Baby.

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:14.980
Then, Hollywood suddenly stopped talking about
her.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:17.120
Why did she go from in demand to out of the
spotlight?

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:19.360
Here are a few theories.

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:22.060
She won two Oscars too quickly

00:00:22.060 --> 00:00:24.170
They say that winning an Oscar can be a curse.

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:26.320
But winning two in the span of five years?

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:28.320
That's a whole other story.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:32.700
Swank faced backlash in Hollywood after she
won her second Best Actress Oscar in 2005,

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.860
for the Clint Eastwood boxing drama Million
Dollar Baby . Suddenly, the actress had gone

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:41.100
from starring in 90210 to joining the ranks
of fabled two-time winners like Bette Davis

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:42.550
and Vivien Leigh.

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:46.280
And she beat out deserving actresses like
the long-suffering Annette Bening, who still

00:00:46.280 --> 00:00:48.309
has never won an Oscar.

00:00:48.309 --> 00:00:49.320
"Shut up!

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.850
Stop it, you weak...you baby.

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:52.860
Shut up.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:53.870
Shut up!"

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:57.470
Beyond the politics of who deserved what,
Swank was inevitably set up for failure by

00:00:57.470 --> 00:00:58.649
the Academy.

00:00:58.649 --> 00:01:00.899
By age 30, she had far exceeded expectations.

00:01:00.899 --> 00:01:04.020
At that point, there was no place to go but
down.

00:01:04.020 --> 00:01:05.489
She got lucky

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:08.810
The strange thing about Swank's two Oscar
wins is that they were both lightning-in-a-bottle

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:10.550
moments in her career.

00:01:10.550 --> 00:01:13.750
She certainly gave excellent performances
in both Boys Don't Cry and Million Dollar

00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:14.750
Baby.

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:18.670
Nobody's going to look back and say to themselves,
"Wait, she won an Oscar for THAT?"

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:19.670
We're looking at you, Marisa Tomei.

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:21.900
"I'm watching you go down in flames.

00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:25.580
And you're bringing me with you, and I can't
do anything about it."

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:29.470
The problem is, outside of those two movies,
Swank has never done anything that warranted

00:01:29.470 --> 00:01:30.890
that level of acclaim.

00:01:30.890 --> 00:01:34.960
Before Boys Don't Cry, she was only really
known for movies like The Next Karate Kid

00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:36.920
and getting fired from 90210.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:40.770
And afterward she continued to star in mediocre
projects like Iron Jawed Angels and The Affair

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:42.340
of the Necklace.

00:01:42.340 --> 00:01:43.380
So was she great?

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:46.560
Or did she just happen to appear in great
movies?

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.890
Lousy post-Oscar Projects

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:53.230
Swank had a difficult time finding success
after Million Dollar Baby was released in

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:54.230
2004.

00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:57.560
She alternated wildly between starring in
prestige movies that were too mediocre for

00:01:57.560 --> 00:02:01.409
Oscar consideration and mainstream flicks
that seemed more geared towards making money

00:02:01.409 --> 00:02:02.960
than satisfying critics.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:07.680
One of her roughest patches came in 2009,
when she starred in the Amelia Earhart biopic

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:11.110
Amelia, which was in every sense of the word
a disaster.

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:14.830
Reviews were terrible—it earned a 20 percent
score on Rotten Tomatoes—and box office

00:02:14.830 --> 00:02:16.340
returns were even worse.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:21.950
The film grossed just $14.2 million against
a reported $40 million budget, and Swank's

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:24.670
days of Oscar glory started to feel like a
thing of the past.

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:26.450
Not that it bothers her.

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:29.880
She told The Independent that, "You're an
artist and you're putting yourself out there,

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:33.560
and you take that leap, and sometimes you
fly and sometimes you fall."

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:35.040
She has other interests

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:38.340
Part of the reason reason Swank hasn’t been
making as many great films is because she’s

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:42.100
devoting her time to helping needy kids and
animals with her non-profit charity.

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:43.560
"It's called the Hilary Foundation.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:48.620
And it brings animals, who have been abandoned,
and children, who've been given up on, together

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:52.360
to help heal each other—through rescue,
rehabilitation, and responsibility training."

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:55.620
It’s all part of her new focus on living
every moment of life to the fullest.

00:02:55.629 --> 00:02:58.530
"You know you just never know what's going
to happen tomorrow, and you have to be really

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:01.390
in the moment and be present."

00:03:01.390 --> 00:03:03.370
She's been caring for her dad

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:06.690
Swank's absence from the spotlight has also
had to do with family concerns beyond her

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:08.230
own control.

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:12.030
In 2015, the actress revealed to the Huffington
Post that she had recently put her career

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:16.099
on the back burner in order to take care of
her ailing father, who had just had a lung

00:03:16.099 --> 00:03:17.099
transplant.

00:03:17.099 --> 00:03:23.170
"It's a certain amount of time, and in a lifespan
it's a blink of an eye, and I wouldn't change

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:24.170
it for the world.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:27.730
I mean there have been job opportunities that
I passed on, things that I said I can't.

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:30.489
But really what we're here for is our family,
right?

00:03:30.489 --> 00:03:33.970
Luckily, her father is on the mend, giving
Swank a chance to get back in the movie business

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:36.980
and hopefully regain her role as one of Hollywood's
top actresses.

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:39.510
Does she have a future on TV?

00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:44.909
Soon, Swank will test her luck on television
again with the new Starz drama The One Percent.

00:03:44.909 --> 00:03:48.569
The premiere date is still up in the air,
but it already boasts a slew of high-profile

00:03:48.569 --> 00:03:53.379
names, including Ed Helms, Ed Harris, and
Oscar-winning director Alejandro G. Inarritu,

00:03:53.379 --> 00:03:55.280
who created and co-wrote the project.

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:56.879
So don't worry too much about Swank.

00:03:56.879 --> 00:04:00.989
Her Oscar days may or may not be behind her,
but her Emmy days could be just starting...

00:04:00.989 --> 00:04:02.189
Thanks for watching!

00:04:02.189 --> 00:04:05.260
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:08.160
And leave us a comment to let us know what
kind of roles you think Hollywood should find

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:08.860
for Hilary Swank...

